  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 302 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nHigh-based/virga showers will be possible over the I-25 corridor \nlate this afternoon (4pm-9pm) as southwesterly flow aloft begins to \nadvect Pacific moisture into our mid levels. Given the large surface \ndewpoint depressions, gusty outflow winds up to 45 mph will be \npossible with the shower activity. A few showers or an isolated \nstorm may linger over the northeast Colorado plains late tonight \nwith gusty winds being the main threat.\n\nTomorrow will be the start of an active weather week with daily \nprecipitation chances through Thursday evening. An upper level \ntrough will move over Southern California Monday, strengthening \nsouthwest flow aloft. Precipitable water (PW) values will reach 200% \nof climatology as early as tomorrow afternoon. While we do not \nexpect the heaviest rainfall to occur tomorrow, scattered showers \nwill develop over the mountains in the morning spreading over the \nDenver metro by the afternoon. Showers tomorrow afternoon will be \nlight and more stratiform-like in nature due to limited instability, \nalthough pockets of brief heavy rain and a few lightning strikes \ncannot be totally ruled out. Light showers will linger into the \nevening hours with similar shower activity expected to continue \nTuesday.\n\nA cold front and thick cloud cover on Tuesday will keep temperatures \nquite fall-like, with highs only reaching the low 70s at low \nelevations and 50s in the mountains. Shower coverage should become \nmore widespread Tuesday evening, and this is where uncertainty \nenters the picture as the highly anticipated upper level trough \nreaches the Four Corners. Model guidance shows quite a bit of \nspread in solutions, indicating that uncertainty still remains. \nHowever, there has been a slight westward shift of the highest QPF\namounts, particularly with Euro deterministic and ensemble \nsolutions. The GFS/GEFS continues to be on the drier side for our \nCWA, but the latest cycles has shifted to include more of \nsoutheast Colorado under the QPF footprint. As the previous AFD \nstated, the largest difference in model solutions seems to be tied\nto the location of the surface low and its relationship to latent\nheat release from shower development. The model solutions that \nbring intense rainfall closer to the Denver metro develop the \nsurface low over eastern Colorado, with drier solutions delaying \nthe development of the low over west-central Kansas. Additionally,\nthere is better consensus that the timing of the heaviest \nrainfall and at least some localized flash flooding concerns would\nbe Wednesday as the trough phases with the remnants of Hurricane \nPolo and moisture becomes wrapped around the new surface low. \nConfidence in more substantial rain amounts for southeast \nColorado, and potentially parts of our southeastern CWA (i.e. \nLincoln and Elbert counties) is also increasing. \n\nWhile mountain snow chances with this system remain low (<30%), it \nis worth mentioning that a westward shift in the bulk of the \nmoisture (such as what the latest Euro guidance has suggested) would \nincrease the potential for some dustings or light snow accumulations \nif temperatures are able to cool down enough Wednesday afternoon \nthrough Thursday morning, mainly above 11,000 ft. Looking ahead to\nthe weekend, long-range ensembles are in better agreement that \ndrier weather will return as ridging prevails over the western \nUnited States. Temperatures look to remain near or below \nseasonable averages over the next ten days.",
